# The operating system as the runtime reaches it (D-138): every module that calls it asks
# `system.linux()`, a constant of the target, and takes the Windows path (kernel32's functions)
# or the Linux path (raw system calls). What many modules share is here: memory from the
# system, Linux's clocks and sleep, and what a Linux process is started with (argc, argv, and
# envp on its first stack).
#
# This module is generated from tools/kernel.dsl by tools/build.py.

kernel is module
kernel purpose "The operating system as the runtime reaches it: Windows through kernel32, Linux through system calls; memory from the system, Linux's clocks, and what a Linux process is started with"
kernel path kernel
kernel imports standard.machine
kernel imports standard.system
kernel imports standard.math
kernel imports standard.compare
kernel imports standard.logic
kernel imports standard.test
kernel exports stateAt
kernel exports initialStack
kernel exports argumentCount
kernel exports argumentVector
kernel exports environmentVector
kernel exports failed
kernel exports errnoOf
kernel exports mapMemory
kernel exports mapStack
kernel exports unmapMemory
kernel exports monotonic
kernel exports wallClock
kernel exports sleepFor

memoryAccess is capability
memoryAccess purpose "Authority to keep what the process was started with in machine.state"
memoryAccess grants use machine.memory none

systemCalls is capability
systemCalls purpose "Authority to take memory from the operating system, give it back, read its clocks, and sleep"
systemCalls grants use system.calls none

# D-138: every module that reaches the operating system asks `system.posix()`, a constant of the target, and takes
# the Windows path (kernel32's functions) or the Linux path (system calls); the backend drops the other. The calls
# many modules share are here. A Linux system call gives a negative errno when it fails (from -4095 to -1).
# machine.state bytes 832 to 895 belong to this module: 832 the address the Linux process started with its stack
# at (0 under `refined run`, and on Windows), 840 argc, 848 argv, 856 envp, 864 to 879 a struct timespec.
stackAt is constant
stackAt purpose "Where the address of the stack the Linux process started with is kept, in machine.state"
stackAt type Int64
stackAt value 832

argcAt is constant
argcAt purpose "Where argc is kept, in machine.state"
argcAt type Int64
argcAt value 840

argvAt is constant
argvAt purpose "Where argv is kept, in machine.state"
argvAt type Int64
argvAt value 848

envpAt is constant
envpAt purpose "Where envp is kept, in machine.state"
envpAt type Int64
envpAt value 856

timeAt is constant
timeAt purpose "Where a struct timespec is put, in machine.state"
timeAt type Int64
timeAt value 864

